<.shell current={:card}>
  <h1>Card</h1>
  <p class="lead">A box for one topic: on a dashboard, or the details under a result.</p>

  <div class="demo">
    <section class="card">
      <header>
        <h2 class="chart-title">Team records</h2>
        <span class="hint">Last refreshed today at 02:00</span>
      </header>
      <p class="paragraph">833 members, 1,912 activities.</p>
      <.button size={:sm}>Refresh now</.button>
    </section>
  </div>

  <h2>When to use</h2>
  <p>
    Rarely. A heading and space group things on almost every page. A box earns its place only where space can't say where one thing ends:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Tiles in a grid, such as a dashboard or the year page, with neighbours on more than one side and uneven heights.
    </li>
    <li>
      Something set apart from the page around it, such as the details of a verify result under its <a href={
        page_path(:band)
      }>band</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When not to use</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      To group a page's sections, a form's, or a sidebar's. A sidebar beside the main column is apart already.
    </li>
    <li>Two topics in one box with a rule between them. That is two boxes, or none.</li>
    <li>A box inside a box.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The test: take the box away. If you can still tell where each part starts and ends, leave it out.
  </p>

  <h2>In the app</h2>
  <p>
    <code>&lt;section class="card"&gt;</code>, with the title and a note in its <code>&lt;header&gt;</code>.
    Cards on a dashboard sit in a <code>.card-grid</code>. Inside a card,
    <code>.card-section</code>
    starts a part under a rule. <code>.card-alt</code>
    greys it, for a box above a form that fills the form in, such as Add activity's template.
  </p>
</.shell>
